    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d350">

                <pb facs="0565" n="463" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg463"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">150.071 Control/205</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Chief of the Division of Western
                        European Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Moffat</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-06-13">June 13, 1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Netherland Minister called this afternoon to renew his protest against the
                    King-Dies Bill. I got in touch with Mr. Simmons, of the Visa Division, who
                    informed me that the Bill had been favorably reported to the Senate and to the
                    House on June 10. He said the Department had reiterated its opposition and that
                    Mr. Carr<anchor n="10" xml:id="d350-f1"/> had as in the past testified against
                    it. We could not predict the ultimate fate but we must bear in mind that this
                    session will last through next-year and that the measure was liable to be called
                    to the floor at any time. The Minister remarked that in that case everything
                    possible would appear to have been done but that the Bill, if passed, would have
                    disastrous results on their shipping.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Pierrepont Moffat</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d350-f1" n="10" xml:id="d350-f1-content">
                <p>Wilbur J. Carr, Assistant Secretary of State.</p>
            </note>
